  without touching storage or regenerating data.
  [3mCosts:[0m one extra indirection, and unread state needs a seen-set rather than a boolean. Recomputing per render is free
  at this scale and would not be at real scale.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate, barely.

  [1mC. Task revision history + diffing.[0m Store full task versions; derive events by diffing consecutive revisions.
  [3mWins when:[0m you need "what exactly changed" detail views, undo, or audit.
  [3mCosts:[0m the heaviest model, and diffing is the wrong problem to be solving here. You'd spend your effort on version
  reconciliation instead of on notification design.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high.

  [1mMy recommendation: B.[0m You told me this is a prototype to make the concept tangible — which means the most valuable
  property is [3mcheap reversibility of the idea[0m, not runtime efficiency. B is the only one where "actually, what if
  notifications worked like GitHub's?" is a ten-line change rather than a rebuild. A is the better production choice and
  the wrong prototype choice; C solves a problem you don't have.

  One caveat, stated plainly: B's advantage is real only if you actually intend to iterate on the rules. If you're
  confident the watcher rule is final and you just want it on screen, A is less machinery and I'd switch my
  recommendation.
